Phase II objective:
Compose executable workflow draft from Phase I atoms.

Phase II composition expectations:
1) Use only Phase I atoms as building blocks.
2) Compose the minimal executable graph needed to satisfy task intent.
3) Keep flow linear unless task intent explicitly needs branching, loops, waits, or time gates.
4) Use one-step-one-purpose structure:
   - `wait_for_event` wakes on one canonical `event_name`.
   - `wait_gate` wakes on one-or-more canonical `subscriptions`.
   - `action` performs manager work and emits declared outputs.
   - `decision` routes on materialized facts.
   - `end` terminates path.
5) Keep manager boundaries clear: split action steps only when manager surface changes or control boundaries are needed.
6) Make step dataflow explicit:
   - `consumes_data_ids` = ids this step reads.
   - `produces_data_ids` = ids this action materializes for later steps.
   - Use deterministic ids from Phase I (`fact_*`, `artifact_*`).
   - For manager action steps, prefer `artifact_*` outputs by default.
   - Use `fact_*` outputs only when a downstream decision/gate explicitly consumes that fact.
   - Simplicity rule: each action step may produce at most one `artifact_*` id.
7) Write decision conditions using only deterministic ids:
   - event booleans: `event_<n> == True/False`
   - facts: `fact_<n>` predicates
   - artifacts only when scalar-safe: `artifact_<n>` predicates
8) Use runtime-parseable expressions only:
   - booleans `True` / `False`
   - quoted string literals
   - no function/method calls (`len(...)`, `.find(...)`, etc.)
9) Keep `wait_for_event.clear_condition` empty in Phase II; runtime/post owns normalization.
10) For `wait_gate`, provide `release_condition` and leave `clear_condition` empty.
11) For step-local relative timers, use canonical `clock.timer.Ns|Nm|Nh`.
12) Do not include watcher registration payloads; post-processing injects those deterministically.
13) Ensure every decision has both `on_true` and `on_false` with different targets.
14) Ensure every non-terminal path reaches an `end`.
15) Binding rules:
   - `wait_for_event`: exactly one `atomic_type=event` binding per step.
   - `wait_gate`: one or more `atomic_type=event` bindings per step.

Additional output requirements:
- Populate `compiled_task` as workflow draft only:
  - `entry_step_id`
  - `steps`
- Populate `logic_tree` so phase composition is explicit:
  - `entry_step_id`: workflow entry step id,
  - `edges`: all directed edges used by the logic graph,
  - `bindings`: map each relevant wait/decision/action step to a Phase I atomic id.
- `bindings.atomic_id` must reference Phase I ids exactly (for example `event_1`, `condition_2`, `action_2`).
